Original work copyright (c) 2026 Luigi C. MIT license, see LICENSE.

src/usb.c and src/chips/mt7921_boot.c adapt the existing BSD-3-Clause-Clear
userland MT76 transport in this research project. Retained copyright notices,
conditions and disclaimer are in LICENSES/BSD-3-Clause-Clear.txt. Source lineage:
https://github.com/openwrt/mt76/tree/c5a3bd91aa735b669618610d5f0ebfa5786845a6

The high-level API takes design inspiration from librtlsdr. No librtlsdr
implementation is incorporated: https://github.com/osmocom/rtl-sdr

The PHY gain-register layout was a research lead from w1700k-sdr, subsequently
checked against this device's ROM and hardware. That chipset's calibration
tables are not included: https://github.com/h0m3us3r/w1700k-sdr/tree/7f151711eede9bdc0e17763c54bd6f2dc1d10662

libusb is a separate dependency under LGPL-2.1-or-later. The library does not
embed libusb, NumPy, Python, a vendor ROM dump, or vendor RAM/patch firmware.
The original device program calls immutable factory-ROM routines already present
in the chip. No nonvolatile programming is implemented.

Fork additions (the SoapySDR module, chain selection, packet-detect
suppression, channel filter, 20/80 MSps, dB gain, birria_ble, tools/decoders/,
tools/research_probes/ and docs/findings.md) are by the contributors in this
repository's Git history, under the same MIT license.

soapy/tests/soapy_rate_test.py, soapy_tune_test.py and osmosdr_test.py adapt
scripts from Mtk79ICAP's userspace/SoapyMT7921/, MIT license, copyright (c) 2026
the MtkWifiRev contributors; see LICENSES/MIT-Mtk79ICAP.txt. The 2.4 GHz BW80
register recipe, the per-chain gain-hold layout and the ICAP tap 12/13
interpretation were research leads from Mtk79ICAP's driver/mt7921/iqstream.c,
re-implemented here and checked on hardware: https://github.com/MtkWifiRev/Mtk79ICAP

tools/decoders/ needs NumPy, SciPy and Matplotlib, which are separate
dependencies under their own licenses. No captured I/Q, vendor firmware or ROM
image is included.
